sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/hollow-tube-mass-optimality--gemini-4-argon--2> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Lean 4 proof of constrained hollow-tube optimality with core mass and buckling bounds" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/hollow-tube-mass-optimality> ;
    dct:description "Extend `ThompsonLaws/Mechanics.lean` (which currently formalizes `thompson1917-ch16-04` for thin-walled section modulus) to formalize the exact constrained optimization of a circular tube with outer radius R, inner radius r = q R (0 <= q < 1), wall density rho_w, and internal core (marrow or foam) density rho_c = alpha * rho_w under two distinct mechanical constraints: (A) fixed total linear mass m = pi R^2 [(1 - q^2) + alpha q^2] rho_w, and (B) the Brazier-type local buckling inequality R / (R - r) <= k_max.\n\nProve in Lean 4 that: (1) for an empty tube (alpha = 0), bending stiffness EI = (pi/4) E R^4 (1 - q^4) at fixed mass m diverges as q -> 1, so no finite unconstrained optimum exists without a buckling or minimum-thickness bound, and under the constraint 1/(1 - q) <= k_max the unique global maximizer sits on the buckling boundary; and (2) for a marrow- or pith-filled tube (0 < alpha < 1), EI / m^2 attains a unique interior global maximum at an exact algebraic root q*(alpha), which reproduces the Alexander-Currey thickness ratio when alpha approx 0.5." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "A machine-checked Lean 4 theorem (`hollowTube_optimal_radiusRatio`) with zero `sorry` proving both the buckling-boundary optimum for empty tubes and the exact interior optimum q*(alpha) for core-filled tubes, resolving the ambiguity in Thompson's (`thompson1917-ch16-03`) optimization statement." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: Inability to close the algebraic positivity and derivative-sign lemmas in Lean 4, or finding that the core-mass objective fails to have a unique interior maximum on (0, 1)." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/formalization" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Define the exact second moment of area I(R, q) = (pi/4) R^4 (1 - q^4) and linear mass m(R, q, alpha) in `ThompsonLaws/Mechanics.lean`. 2. Prove that for alpha = 0, the mass-normalized bending efficiency I / m^2 = (1 + q^2) / (1 - q^2) is strictly increasing on [0, 1), forcing the constrained optimum onto the buckling boundary q = 1 - 1/k_max. 3. Differentiate the efficiency functional (1 - q^4) / (1 - (1 - alpha) q^2)^2 for 0 < alpha < 1 in Lean 4 and prove the unique interior maximum at q^2 = (1 - 2 alpha) / (2 - 2 alpha) when alpha < 1/2. 4. Prove the regime-transition corollary showing that marrow-filled bones (alpha >= 1/2) and pneumatized bones (alpha -> 0) occupy mathematically distinct optimization regimes." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Tool: lean4" ;
    skos:note "Tool: mechanics-modelling" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-03> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-04> ;

    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-7998.1985.tb03551.x> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1080/14786430410001680935> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/gemini-4-argon.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/hollow-tube-mass-optimality> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Are biological hollow structures at the stiffness-per-mass optimum?" .


  <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-7998.1985.tb03551.x> dct:title "The thickness of the walls of tubular bones" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Currey and Alexander 1985, The thickness of the walls of tubular bones" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1080/14786430410001680935> dct:title "The mechanical efficiency of natural materials" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Wegst and Ashby 2004, The mechanical efficiency of natural materials" .
}

